  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>San Antonio building codes are the city's adopted model codes plus local amendments, enforced by the <a href="https://www.sa.gov/Directory/Departments/DSD">San Antonio Development Services Department (DSD)</a>. If you are building a new home inside city limits, DSD reviews your plans, issues your permits and sends the inspectors. Here is what the codes cover, how permitting and zoning work, and the local issues that trip up new builders.</p>

<h2>Why the codes matter</h2>
<p>Codes set the minimum standard for a safe, durable house. Following them:</p>
<ul>
<li>Protects your family through sound structure, fire safety and safe wiring</li>
<li>Keeps financing, insurance and resale on track</li>
<li>Lowers energy and water bills through efficiency requirements</li>
<li>Reduces your liability if something goes wrong later</li>
</ul>

<h2>Which codes apply</h2>
<p>For a single-family home, the core document is the International Residential Code (IRC), with companion electrical, plumbing, mechanical and energy codes. Commercial and larger multifamily projects fall under <a href="https://codes.iccsafe.org/content/IBC2021P2">the International Building Code (IBC)</a>. San Antonio amends these model codes for local conditions such as heat, storms and flooding. Edition updates happen every few years, so confirm the current editions and amendments with DSD before your plans are finalized.</p>

<h2>The building permit process</h2>
<h3>When you need a permit</h3>
<ul>
<li>New homes and additions</li>
<li>Structural renovations or added square footage</li>
<li>Electrical, plumbing and mechanical work</li>
<li>Many fences above certain heights, decks, carports and pools</li>
</ul>
<p>Cosmetic work such as painting or replacing flooring usually does not need one, but check if you are unsure.</p>
<h3>How to apply</h3>
<ol>
<li>Identify your permit type (new residential, addition, trade).</li>
<li>Submit your application online through the <a href="https://www.sa.gov/Directory/Departments/DSD/BuildSA">BuildSA portal</a>.</li>
<li>Upload a site plan, construction drawings, energy compliance and contractor details.</li>
<li>Respond to plan review comments and pay fees, which vary with project scope.</li>
<li>Schedule inspections at each stage of construction.</li>
</ol>
<p>Fees and review times change; check the current schedule with DSD rather than relying on old numbers. Incomplete submittals are the most common reason for slow approvals, so double-check that your site plan shows setbacks, easements, drainage and impervious cover, and that your foundation and framing details are stamped where the city requires an engineer.</p>

<h2>Zoning and land use</h2>
<p>San Antonio has zoning, governed by its Unified Development Code. Residential districts such as R-4, R-6 and R-20 set minimum lot sizes, setbacks and other limits, and historic districts and overlays add design review. Look up your lot on the <a href="https://www.sa.gov/Directory/Departments/DSD/Constructing/Land-Development/Zoning">San Antonio zoning maps and ordinances</a> pages before you design. If your plans do not fit current zoning, a variance or rezoning goes through a public process; the <a href="https://www.sa.gov/Directory/Departments/DSD/Boards-Commissions/Zoning-Commission">San Antonio Zoning Commission</a> handles zoning cases. Our guide to <a href="/choosing-the-right-location-for-your-texas-dream-home/">choosing the right location for your Texas home</a> covers other lot checks.</p>

<h2>Inspections and compliance</h2>
<p>Once permitted, your project is inspected at key points:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Foundation:</strong> footing depth, reinforcement or cables, and forms</li>
<li><strong>Framing:</strong> structure, connectors and fireblocking before drywall</li>
<li><strong>Electrical, plumbing and mechanical rough-ins</strong></li>
<li><strong>Final:</strong> confirms the finished home matches approved plans and code</li>
</ul>
<p>You can <a href="https://www.sa.gov/Directory/Departments/DSD/Constructing/Business-Commercial/Inspections">schedule a San Antonio building inspection</a> through the city. Missing an inspection can mean opening up finished work, so build inspections into your schedule. An illustrated reference like [[amz:books_code_check|Code Check]] makes pre-inspection walks easier.</p>
<!--products:books_code_check,books_code_check_electrical,laser_measure|References and tools for pre-inspection walks-->
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> San Antonio lots often hit limestone close to the surface while others sit on expansive clay, sometimes in the same subdivision. Get a soil report before your engineer designs the foundation, and budget for rock excavation if plumbing trenches will hit limestone.</p></div>

<h2>Floodplain, stormwater and water rules</h2>
<p>Parts of San Antonio flood fast during heavy rain. If your lot is in a FEMA-mapped floodplain, you will need a floodplain development permit, elevation documentation and drainage plans; review the <a href="https://docsonline.sanantonio.gov/FileUploads/dsd/flooplain_permit.pdf">San Antonio floodplain development requirements</a>. Flood insurance itself is usually bought through the National Flood Insurance Program or private carriers via an insurance agent, while the <a href="https://www.twdb.texas.gov/">Texas Water Development Board (TWDB)</a> coordinates floodplain management and flood information at the state level. Lots over the Edwards Aquifer recharge zone can also face extra environmental rules.</p>

<h2>Energy efficiency and green building</h2>
<p>San Antonio enforces energy code requirements for insulation, windows, air sealing and HVAC efficiency. The U.S. Department of Energy's <a href="https://www.energycodes.gov/">energy codes site</a> explains the International Energy Conservation Code (IECC) that these local rules are based on. The city has also promoted voluntary green building through <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/about/build-san-antonio-green">Build San Antonio Green and related programs</a>, and local utilities have offered rebates for efficient homes; check what is available now. See our guide to <a href="/energy-efficient-home-building-in-the-texas-heat/">energy-efficient building in the Texas heat</a>.</p>

<h2>Apartments, multifamily and insurance claims</h2>
<p>Multifamily and commercial projects carry extra requirements for fire sprinklers, alarms, exits and accessibility under the <a href="https://www.ada.gov/">ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act)</a>. Owners of apartment properties should also understand insurance claims after storm or fire damage; a <a href="https://txpublicadjusting.com/commercial-claims/texas-public-adjuster-for-apartment-complex/">licensed public adjuster for apartment complexes</a> can help document losses and work with the carrier.</p>

<h2>Quick compliance checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>Confirm the lot is inside city limits and check its zoning district.</li>
<li>Check historic district, overlay, floodplain and aquifer status.</li>
<li>Order a soil report before the foundation is designed.</li>
<li>Submit complete plans through BuildSA and answer every review comment.</li>
<li>Hire state-licensed electricians, plumbers and HVAC contractors for trade permits.</li>
<li>Schedule each inspection and never cover work that has not passed.</li>
<li>Keep permits, inspection results and photos in one binder for your lender, insurer and future buyers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Check your zoning, flood status and soil, then contact the <a href="https://www.sa.gov/Directory/Departments/DSD">San Antonio Development Services Department</a> to confirm current code editions, submittal requirements and fees. Compare other metros in our <a href="/navigating-texas-building-codes-what-you-need-to-know/">Texas building codes overview</a>, and plan the whole project with our <a href="/free-training/">free training</a>.</p>
